CREATE OR REPLACE FUNCTION cdc.apply_orders_cdc(p_limit INT DEFAULT 100)
RETURNS INT
LANGUAGE plpgsql AS $$
DECLARE
  v_last BIGINT;
  v_applied INT := 0;
  r RECORD;
BEGIN
  SELECT last_seq
  INTO STRICT v_last
  FROM cdc.cdc_consumer_offset
  WHERE consumer_name = 'orders_search'
  FOR UPDATE;

  IF p_limit IS NULL OR p_limit <= 0 THEN
    RETURN 0;
  END IF;

  FOR r IN
    SELECT seq, op, order_id, email, status, total_cents, updated_at
    FROM cdc.cdc_orders_changelog
    WHERE seq > v_last
    ORDER BY seq
    LIMIT p_limit
  LOOP
    IF r.op = 'delete' THEN
      DELETE FROM cdc.orders_search
      WHERE order_id = r.order_id;
    ELSE
      INSERT INTO cdc.orders_search (
        order_id, email, status, total_cents, updated_at
      )
      VALUES (
        r.order_id, r.email, r.status, r.total_cents, r.updated_at
      )
      ON CONFLICT (order_id) DO UPDATE
      SET email = EXCLUDED.email,
          status = EXCLUDED.status,
          total_cents = EXCLUDED.total_cents,
          updated_at = EXCLUDED.updated_at;
    END IF;

    v_last := r.seq;
    v_applied := v_applied + 1;
  END LOOP;

  IF v_applied > 0 THEN
    UPDATE cdc.cdc_consumer_offset
    SET last_seq = v_last
    WHERE consumer_name = 'orders_search';
  END IF;

  RETURN v_applied;
END;
$$;
```

The `FOR UPDATE` lock serializes consumers on the `orders_search` checkpoint. Each caller reads its starting sequence only after acquiring that lock, applies at most `p_limit` events in ascending sequence order, and advances the checkpoint only to the highest sequence it actually applied. Projection mutations and checkpoint advancement occur in the same transaction. Upserts and deletes are idempotent, so retrying delivery is safe; if no events exist, the function returns `0` without changing the checkpoint.

Writing the source row and its outbox event in the same database transaction removes the application-level dual-write failure window: either both commit or both roll back. A separate consumer still delivers committed outbox events to the projection asynchronously, so retries and temporary lag remain possible and must be tolerated.

This is a trigger-based transactional outbox, not PostgreSQL logical-decoding CDC. The trigger explicitly inserts application-shaped event rows into a normal outbox table during source-table DML, and the consumer queries that table. Logical decoding instead derives changes from PostgreSQL's write-ahead log through a replication slot and decoding plugin.

